<!-- FAQ -->
<section aria-labelledby="faq">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <div class="faq">
<details><summary><span>What is 10xGraph?</span><svg class="chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 6l5 5 5-5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/></svg></summary><p>10xGraph is an open-source Python framework for building production multi-agent AI systems. You write agents as typed StateGraphs or with the prebuilt ReactAgent, and 10xGraph generates the production server around them: REST and streaming endpoints, JWT auth, owner-only threads, per-tool permissions, rate limits, and Docker and Kubernetes manifests. It is MIT licensed and made by 10xScale.</p></details>
<details><summary><span>What happens if a run crashes mid-way?</span><svg class="chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 6l5 5 5-5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/></svg></summary><p>With a checkpointer configured, 10xGraph records the current node before running it and keeps a ledger of every completed tool call. When the run resumes, the interrupted node runs again, but any tool call already in the ledger is skipped instead of executed. A payment tool that finished before the crash does not charge the card a second time.</p></details>
<details><summary><span>How does 10xGraph handle authentication and multi-user isolation?</span><svg class="chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 6l5 5 5-5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/></svg></summary><p>The API server supports JWT or a custom auth class. The production template sets authorization to ownership, so a thread is accessible only to the user who created it, and owner checks are cached in process and in Redis. You can also restrict individual tools by role and add rate limits backed by memory or Redis.</p></details>
<details><summary><span>How is 10xGraph different from LangGraph?</span><svg class="chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 6l5 5 5-5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/></svg></summary><p>Both model agents as graphs. With 10xGraph, the API server, authentication, rate limits and Docker and Kubernetes manifests come from the same MIT-licensed install, and you host them yourself. LangGraph offers its managed server through LangSmith Deployment. LangGraph also requires langchain-core, while 10xGraph has no LangChain dependency.</p></details>
<details><summary><span>Which LLM providers does 10xGraph support?</span><svg class="chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 6l5 5 5-5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/></svg></summary><p>10xGraph picks the provider from the model string. It supports OpenAI, Google Gemini including Vertex AI, and Anthropic through the direct API, Vertex AI or Amazon Bedrock. Any OpenAI-compatible endpoint also works. Changing the model string does not change your graph, your tools or your API endpoints.</p></details>
<details><summary><span>Is 10xGraph free and open source?</span><svg class="chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 6l5 5 5-5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/></svg></summary><p>Yes. 10xGraph is released under the MIT license, including the API server and the CLI. You can use it in commercial products, self-host it on your own infrastructure and modify it. There is no hosted platform you have to buy and no account to create before you start building.</p></details>
<details><summary><span>Is 10xGraph production-ready?</span><svg class="chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 6l5 5 5-5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/></svg></summary><p>10xScale runs its own AI products on 10xGraph in production. It includes replay-safe tool calls, versioned state writes, node and tool timeouts, JWT auth, owner-only threads, rate limiting and Kubernetes manifests. The core is at version 0.9.2, before 1.0, so pin your versions and read the changelog when you upgrade.</p></details>
<details><summary><span>How do I install 10xGraph?</span><svg class="chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 6l5 5 5-5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/></svg></summary><p>Run pip install 10xgraph 10xgraph-api on Python 3.12 or newer. Then run 10xgraph init --yes --template production to scaffold a project with auth, validators, tests and config, 10xgraph api to serve it on port 8000, and 10xgraph build --docker-compose --k8s to generate deployment files. The TypeScript client installs with npm install 10xgraph-client.</p></details>
<details><summary><span>Was 10xGraph previously called Agentflow?</span><svg class="chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 6l5 5 5-5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/></svg></summary><p>Yes. 10xGraph was published as Agentflow, under the PyPI name 10xscale-agentflow, until 2026. The project was renamed because many unrelated tools share the Agentflow name. The Python import path stays the same, so existing code that imports from agentflow keeps working after you switch to the new package names.</p></details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

